-- title:
--   Fine Čerednik–Drinfeld family depends on ψ only through Frobenius invariants
-- statement:
--   The statement is set in the full Čerednik–Drinfeld frame for fake elliptic curves, and its conclusion concerns the uniformizing family $\Theta_f$ on $\pi$-nilpotent algebras.
--
--   **Arithmetic data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a nonzero natural number $N$ with $r \nmid N$ and $\bar r \nmid N$.
--
--   **The base ring.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi$, completeness of $\mathcal O$ for the $\pi$-adic topology, residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); $K_0$ is a characteristic-zero fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The unramified cover and its Frobenius.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r \pmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). Further, a monoid homomorphism $v\det \colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^n$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The indefinite quaternion algebra and the coarse moduli scheme.** Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ the algebra $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes \mathbb Q_v$ is a division algebra precisely when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ (an order not properly contained in another order). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment `pt` sending each ring $S$, each morphism $s \colon \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and each fake elliptic curve of level $N$ over $S$ to a point of $\mathcal X$ over $s$, such that `h𝒳` holds: `pt` is invariant under isomorphism, compatible with base change along ring maps, bijective on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and universal among such assignments.
--
--   **The fine moduli scheme with full level $n$ and its twisting group.** A level $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$; a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with structure morphism $f_M$ and an assignment `ptF` on fake elliptic curves of level $N$ equipped with a full level-$n$ structure, satisfying `hM` (`IsFineModuli`: isomorphism-invariance, compatibility with base change, and surjectivity and injectivity on $S$-points for every ring $S$). A group $G$, a homomorphism $\rho \colon G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and a labelling $\chi \colon G \to \Lambda$ with `hρ` (`IsLevelTwistAction`, seven clauses: each $\rho(g)$ lies over the base; twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ corresponds to composition with $\rho(g)$; and $\chi$ is unital, multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n\Lambda$).
--
--   **Comparison of fine and coarse levels.** A morphism $p \colon M \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $f_M$, invariant under the action of $G$ (`hρp`), and compatible with the two moduli assignments after forgetting the level-$n$ structure (`hp_pt`).
--
--   **Hecke correspondences.** For each prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with an assignment `ptT` on fake elliptic curves of level $N$ with an extra level-$\ell$ structure satisfying `IsCoarseModuliT` (`h𝒴`, the same five clauses as above for those data), together with two morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) \colon \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on points forget the extra structure (`hd₀`) and pass to a level-$\ell$ isogenous curve (`hd₁`). Two further endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) which on points send a curve to any Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$, respectively at $\bar r$ (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **The definite quaternion side.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$, and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]\otimes\mathbb Q_v$ a division algebra exactly at the places above $\bar r$. A maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ (an intersection of two maximal orders of relative index $N$) contained in $\Lambda_1$, a finite-idelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$, with $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ an Eichler order of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`). An injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0 \colon \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$, and a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$.
--
--   **The arithmetic group and its Hecke elements.** A subgroup $\tilde\Gamma$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ whose elements are exactly those of `awayUnits R₁ v`, i.e. those lying, at every finite place $w \neq v$, in the subgroup generated by the local box units of $R_1$ at $w$ (`hΓt`). Elements $s(\ell) \in \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^\times$ and finite ideles $s_f(\ell)$ for each prime $\ell \neq r, \bar r$, subject to `hs` (four clauses): at each place not above $r$ the component of $s_f(\ell)$ is the diagonal image of $s(\ell)$; at each place above $r$ it is $1$; the product of the diagonal idele of $\ell$ with $s_f(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s(\ell)) = \ell$. Subgroups $\tilde\Gamma_\ell = \tilde\Gamma \cap s(\ell)\tilde\Gamma s(\ell)^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). An element $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`). A homomorphism $\theta_t \colon \tilde\Gamma \to G$.
--
--   **The uniformizing family.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_f \colon \bigl((\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \hat\Omega(B)\bigr) \times G \longrightarrow \{\varphi \colon \operatorname{Spec} B \to M \mid \varphi \text{ lies over } \operatorname{Spec} B \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O\},$$
--   where $\hat\Omega(B)$ is the set of Deligne data over $B$ for $(K_0,\pi)$: families of $B$-submodules of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} L$, one for each full $\mathcal O$-lattice $L$ in $K_0^2$, with invertible quotient, compatible with inclusions of lattices, equivariant for homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$. The family is subject to: `hnat`, naturality in $B$ for $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hG`, the identity $\rho(h)_* \Theta_f(x, gh) = \Theta_f(x,g)$; `hinv`, the invariance $\Theta_f(x', \theta_t(\gamma)g) = \Theta_f(x,g)$ whenever $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and $(x,x')$ are related by the twisted action of $\iota_0(\gamma)$, that is $x'_1 = x_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$; `het`, unique lifting along any surjection $B \to B_0$ of $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras whose kernel has zero square, for each datum over $B_0$ and each compatible $B$-point of $M$; and `hgeom`, the description over algebraically closed fields $k$ which are $\pi$-nilpotent $\mathcal O$-algebras: for a fixed $\psi \colon \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$ every point of $M(k)$ is of the form $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$, and $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi'(y) = \psi(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}(y))$ for every $y \in \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ that is fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ (those with $z = c \cdot 1$ for some $c \in \mathbb Q$) satisfying $\theta_t(z) = 1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, all $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphisms $\psi, \psi' \colon \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, every Deligne datum $P$ over $B$ and every $g \in G$: if $\psi(y) = \psi'(y)$ for every $y \in \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ with the property that $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}(y) = y$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $\theta_t(z) = 1$, then
--   $$\Theta_f\bigl((\psi,P),g\bigr) = \Theta_f\bigl((\psi',P),g\bigr).$$
--   Thus the uniformizing family sees the coefficient homomorphism only through its restriction to the subring of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by the Frobenius powers attached to the central elements of $\tilde\Gamma$ in the kernel of $\theta_t$.
--
--   This is one clause in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformization of the fine moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves with full level structure: it extends the coefficient-twisting clause of the geometric-points description `hgeom`, in the case $\gamma = 1$, from algebraically closed fields to arbitrary $\mathcal O$-algebras in which $\pi$ is nilpotent. It is obtained from [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.apply_eq_apply_of_isPullback_of_frobTwist_eq_of_invariant`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.apply_eq_apply_of_isPullback_of_frobTwist_eq_of_invariant), and it feeds the Deligne-datum part of [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_apply_ne_zero_forall_isPullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_apply_ne_zero_forall_isPullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq), where an orbit relation valid at a point is spread over a neighbourhood.
